      { trap: "The most common meeting failure in English is ending without an explicit summary. Everyone leaves with a slightly different memory, and two weeks later there is a disagreement about what was agreed. Sixty seconds of *so to summarise* is worth more than the previous fifty-nine minutes." },

      {
        tryit: {
          t: "Get into a fast conversation",
          task: "Three colleagues are talking quickly about a decision you think rests on a wrong assumption. Write three ways in — soft, neutral and firm — that each take under five seconds to say.",
          hint: "The signal word comes before the content. Names work. So does *before we move on*.",
          sol: { lang: "text", code: "Soft:     Sorry - can I jump in for a second?\nNeutral:  Before we move on, can I check one thing?\nFirm:     Hold on - I think we are assuming the vendor API supports\n          bulk deletes, and I do not think it does." },
          w: "The firm version works because it names the specific assumption immediately. A firm interruption with a vague follow-up is just rude; a firm interruption that immediately justifies itself is welcome."
        }
      }
