  {
    t: "Negotiation, Difficult Conversations and Small Talk",
    m: "speak",
    lvl: "advanced",
    s: "Salary, scope and contract language; the conversation nobody wants to have; and the ten minutes before the meeting starts.",
    goal: [
      "Negotiate using anchoring, silence and conditional language",
      "Open and survive a conversation that has been avoided for weeks",
      "Do small talk in a second language without it feeling like an exam"
    ],
    b: [
      { h: "Negotiation language" },

      { p: "Most negotiation in professional life is not about salary. It is about scope, dates, ownership and resources — and it runs on a small set of linguistic moves." },

      {
        tbl: {
          t: "The core moves",
          h: ["Move", "Language", "Note"],
          rows: [
            ["**Anchor**", "We are looking at something in the region of X.", "The first number stated pulls the range towards it. Whoever anchors first has an advantage when the other side is uncertain."],
            ["**Conditional concession**", "**If** you can do X, **then** I could look at Y.", "Never concede unconditionally. *If–then* keeps every give attached to a get."],
            ["**Range, not a point**", "Somewhere between X and Y, depending on scope.", "Gives room without appearing to move."],
            ["**Justify with a standard**", "Based on what similar roles are paying / based on the last three projects of this size…", "External standards are much harder to argue with than preferences."],
            ["**The flinch**", "*(pause)* That is quite a bit lower than I was expecting.", "A short honest reaction, then silence."],
            ["**Silence**", "*(say nothing)*", "The most underrated tool in negotiation. Most people fill a silence by improving their own offer."],
            ["**Widen the pie**", "If the number is fixed, are there other things we can look at — start date, title, learning budget, review timing?", "Turns a one-dimensional fight into a trade."],
            ["**Close**", "If you can do X, I am ready to say yes today.", "Gives the other side a reason to move: certainty."]
          ]
        }
      },

      { n: "**Never give a number first when asked your current or expected salary**, if you can avoid it: *I would rather understand the scope and the band first — what range is budgeted for the role?* If pushed, give a researched range with a justification attached, and make the bottom of your range a number you would genuinely accept, because that is the number you will get.", nt: "Who names the number" },

      { h: "Phrases that lose money" },

      {
        tbl: {
          t: "Say this, not that",
          h: ["Avoid", "Use", "Why"],
          rows: [
            ["I need at least X.", "I am looking for X, based on [standard].", "*Need* is about you; a standard is about the market."],
            ["Whatever you think is fair.", "What range is budgeted for the role?", "The first sentence guarantees the bottom of the band."],
            ["I am sorry to ask, but…", "*(no apology at all)*", "Apologising for negotiating tells the other side you expect to lose."],
            ["Is there any chance you could…?", "Could you do X?", "*Any chance* pre-concedes."],
            ["I will take it!", "That works for me — could you send the details in writing?", "Enthusiasm is fine; skipping the written confirmation is not."],
            ["That is my final offer.", "That is where I need to be for this to work.", "Ultimatums close doors you may need."]
          ]
        }
      },

      { h: "The difficult conversation" },

      { p: "Underperformance, a missed commitment, a colleague's behaviour, a relationship that has soured. The language matters less than the structure, and the structure has four parts." },

      {
        ol: [
          "**Signal it, briefly.** *I want to talk about something a bit awkward, and I would rather say it directly than let it sit.* Naming the discomfort defuses most of it.",
          "**State the observable facts.** Dates, events, what was said. No motives, no adjectives about the person.",
          "**State the impact on you or on the work.** *The effect is that I am rewriting the estimate every week.*",
          "**Ask.** *What is your read on it?* Then stop talking. The most important part of a difficult conversation is the ninety seconds after your first question."
        ]
      },

      {
        tbl: {
          t: "Openers for conversations people avoid",
          h: ["Situation", "Opener"],
          rows: [
            ["A colleague repeatedly misses commitments", "I want to talk about the last three sprints, and I want to understand it rather than complain about it."],
            ["Someone took credit for your work", "In Thursday's review the dashboard came up as your work. I built it, and I would like that to be clear. Can we correct it together?"],
            ["Your manager is not supporting you", "I want to raise something about how we are working. I have felt unsupported on the Atlas escalation, and I would like to talk about what would be different next time."],
            ["Tension with a peer", "I think we have got on the wrong foot on this project, and I would rather fix it than work around it. Can we talk?"],
            ["You need to end something", "I want to be straight with you about where this is going, because you deserve to hear it from me first."]
          ]
        }
      },

      { trap: "The most common error in a difficult conversation is talking too much, because silence is uncomfortable and you have rehearsed. State your part in under sixty seconds and then genuinely stop. A conversation where you speak for eight minutes is not a conversation; it is a verdict being delivered." },

      { h: "Small talk, for people who find it pointless" },

      { p: "Small talk is not a conversation about the weather. It is a low-stakes protocol for establishing that two people are safe to talk to, and skipping it in most cultures reads as coldness rather than efficiency. Treat it as a professional skill with a script and it stops being an ordeal." },

      {
        tbl: {
          t: "Openers that work and openers that stall",
          h: ["Works", "Stalls", "Why"],
          rows: [
            ["How has your week been so far?", "How are you?", "The second gets *fine* and ends. The first has to be answered with content."],
            ["What are you working on at the moment?", "What do you do?", "The second gets a job title; the first gets something they care about."],
            ["How did you end up in [field]?", "Where do you work?", "Origin stories are the most reliable conversational fuel there is."],
            ["What is keeping you busy outside work?", "Do you have any hobbies?", "*Hobbies* sounds like a form. *Keeping you busy* is natural."],
            ["Have you been to one of these before?", "*(silence)*", "Any question about the shared situation is a valid opener."],
            ["What did you make of the keynote?", "Nice weather.", "A shared experience beats an abstract topic."]
          ]
        }
      },

      {
        tbl: {
          t: "Keeping it going, and getting out",
          h: ["Move", "Say"],
          rows: [
            ["Follow up rather than switch topic", "Oh — what was that like? / How did that come about?"],
            ["Offer something of your own", "*(after they answer, give a comparable detail — a question-only conversation is an interview)*"],
            ["Find the hook", "*(listen for the thing they said with more energy and ask about that)*"],
            ["Exit gracefully", "It was good to meet you — I am going to grab a coffee before the next session."],
            ["Exit with a next step", "I would like to hear more about that — are you on LinkedIn?"],
            ["Rescue a dying conversation", "Anyway — what are you hoping to get out of today?"]
          ]
        }
      },

      { n: "Two safe universal topics: the shared situation (this event, this office, the journey here) and food. Two to avoid with people you do not know: politics and anything that requires knowing someone's family situation. *Do you have kids?* is small talk in some countries and intrusive in others.", nt: "Safe ground" },

      {
        tryit: {
          t: "Handle the salary question",
          task: "A recruiter asks: *What are your current salary and your expectations?* Write a reply that deflects without evading, and a second version for when they insist.",
          hint: "First: turn it into a question about the band. Second: give a justified range whose floor you would accept.",
          sol: { lang: "text", code: "First pass\nI would rather understand the scope before talking numbers - what range\nhas been budgeted for the role?\n\nWhen they insist\nBased on what I have seen for similar platform roles at this level in\nthis market, I am looking at somewhere between X and Y, depending on\nscope and the rest of the package. Does that sit inside your band?\n\n(Then stop talking.)" },
          w: "*Does that sit inside your band?* hands the next move back to them and ends your turn cleanly, which removes the temptation to keep talking and negotiate against yourself."
        }
      }
    ],
    k: [
      "Never concede unconditionally: *if you can do X, then I could look at Y*.",
      "Silence is a negotiation tool. Most people fill it by improving their own offer.",
      "Justify with an external standard rather than a personal need.",
      "Difficult conversations: signal, facts, impact, ask — then stop talking.",
      "Small talk is a protocol, not a conversation. Ask about origin stories and current work, not job titles.",
      "Never apologise for negotiating; it signals you expect to lose."
    ],
    drill: {
      lang: "text",
      reps: 3,
      items: [
        { c: "If you can move the start date, then I could work with that number.", w: "conditional concession - never give without a get" },
        { c: "What range has been budgeted for the role?", w: "avoid naming the first number" },
        { c: "I want to say something directly rather than let it sit.", w: "opening a difficult conversation" },
        { c: "How did you end up in this field?", w: "the most reliable small-talk opener" }
      ]
    }
  }

